---

!! Syntax

```
requirementDiagram
    requirement | functionalRequirement | performanceRequirement
    | interfaceRequirement | designConstraint | physicalRequirement reqId {
        id: REQ-001
        text: Requirement text.
        risk: low | medium | high
        verifymethod: analysis | inspection | test | demonstration
    }
    element elementId {
        type: label
        docRef: optional-reference
    }
    elementId - satisfies | traces | contains | copies
              | refines | verifies | derives -> reqId
```

|!Relationship|!Meaning|
|`satisfies`|Element implements the requirement|
|`traces`|Element is related to the requirement (analysis link)|
|`verifies`|Element is used to verify the requirement|
|`refines`|Element elaborates or details the requirement|
|`derives`|Requirement is derived from another requirement|
|`contains`|Requirement contains a sub-requirement|
|`copies`|Requirement is copied from another source|

!! Tips

* Use `functionalRequirement` and `performanceRequirement` type keywords (not generic `requirement`) to visually distinguish requirement categories in the rendered boxes.
* Keep `text:` values concise — long sentences overflow the requirement box in most themes.
* `verifymethod` accepts `analysis`, `inspection`, `test`, and `demonstration` (ISO 15288 verification methods).
* Requirement IDs (`id:`) are displayed in the box header; use a consistent scheme like `SYS-001`.
* See also: [[Entity Relationship]] for data-centric traceability, [[C4 Diagram]] for system context at a higher level.

